w  turned out to actually be pretty good  not perfect there are problems I'll get  to them but the problems it has aren't  the ones I was expecting it's not dark  just for its own sake it's dark because  that's the element of young adulthood  that it's actively playing to because I  think back on it most of us go through  an angsty nobody understands me phase in  our lives and there's a reason that for  most of us this starts in adolescence  because that's the point where we  realize that the world is harsher than  when we're told when we were younger but  we also don't have the power or  authority to do anything about it we  feel trapped by a world that couldn't  care less about us we self-doubt we  question we get introspective in a way  that tends to lower our self-esteem and  our confidence and that is the aspect of  adolescent  that this show was handling which makes  the darker approach actually fit the  story instead of feeling like a forced  attempt at superficial maturity so if  you're not familiar with the Teen Titans  the team first appeared in the 1960s  with the premise of basically putting  the teen sidekicks together from some of  their own adventures you know various  sidekicks to various adult superheroes  now the original team lineup that  continued on through the 70s but it  isn't really worth dwelling on that too  much because it was the second iteration  that served as the template for the  characters that most folks now know as  the Teen Titans so in 1980 readers were  introduced to the New Teen Titans which  carried over several original members  including Robin but more importantly  brought to the character of Beast Boy  over from the Doom Patrol and introduced  the new characters of cyborg Raven and  Starfire and it's these five that have  become the iconic lineup of the team  they're the ones in both cartoon  versions the characters generally  expected to appear when the name Teen  Titan shows up  now this live-action Titans it doesn't  fea