 in their  episodes well a lot of them they feel  like backdoor pilots spin-offs you know  where we get the hero or the hero team  brought into the story and the show  really does feel like it's got one eye  on the audience going so what do you  think about this one getting their own  series yeah out of the three that are  handled this way one of them is about as  close to seamless as these sort of  things tend to be one is far less  seamless but still pretty enjoyable and  what it's just painful and annoying the  one that comes off the best I would say  is Donna Troy former sidekick to Wonder  Woman and she's brought in when dick  breaks off from the team for for a time  and basically needs somebody to confide  in about the fact that he's having  trouble  leaving Robin behind sometimes you're  confrontational hold on like right now  I'm not wow that was adult  they have good chemistry which helps and  also this whole question has been eating  at him the whole show already so when he  thinks Rachel's basically safe at least  for the time being it makes sense that  he turned to somebody like her for  guidance now a little less cleanly  integrated is the Doom Patrol they do  fit in do gars origin but there's an  entire episode that's basically devoted  to a little more than a Rachel getting  to meet each member of the team in turn  and then leave with gar in tow and so it  really does feel pretty removed from the  ongoing story so what helps is that the  Doom Patrol and the team members various  inherent weirdness is just fun to watch  I'm pretty compelling on its own I mean  when you have a group consisting of a  human mind stuck in a robot a former  movie star who's basically a big blob of  putty and a guy wrapped up in bandages  for reasons that are not immediately  apparent it's not too hard to get swept  up in the madness of it all  it may not push much of the overall  story of the season forward but it's fun  enough to work as a one-off sadly the  same can't be said for Hawk and Dove who  are not only poorly integrated into the  story they're boring annoying have  garishly bright costumes that feel very  out of place in this darker story and  world and eat up way more screen time  than anybody needed this is true of the  duo collectively but doubly true for  Hawk specifically this guy is just kind  of insufferable he's a big meathead with  less brains than muscle and he didn't  have the endearing streak that was  desperately needed to make me not hate  every single second he's on the screen I  know that he's supposed to be balanced  out by Dove but he needs to at least  work slightly as a character on his own  these two they get brought into the  story initially because  dick thinks it might be better to have  somebody else look after Rachel while  he's trying to sort out what's going on  which okay you know what as a concept  fair enough but even before we  understand why these two are here we are  treated to extended sequences of the  pair where I just kept muttering to  myself why am I having to watch these  two doofuses it also doesn't help that  later on they get a devoted origin  episode after being out of the story for  like five episodes and that origin for  them gets dropped immediately following  a cliffhanger ending from the previous  episode so the story that you know we're  invested in gets put on pause so we can  find out where these two morons came  from folks who watched stranger things  may actually get a little bit of deja vu  between this and the much-maligned  Episode seven of season two where the  main story was put on hold for a side  story about an absent character now I  actually didn't mind it in stranger  things because the cliffhanger in that  one was one that I both saw coming in  advance and had to me a pretty obvious  immediate resolution I wasn't you know I  wasn't going wow I didn't know that  would happen and I can't imagine what  comes next  you know so it kind of worked with  stranger things I felt I'm in the  minority on that whatever but here while  I did kind of see the setup for the  twist and the cliffhanger coming it is  still a twist cliffhanger that up ends  the dynamic of the show and puts the  characters in immediate moral danger  that does not have a clear way out this  is not the time to cut away to the  origin of two characters that I had  hoped I would never see again to be fair  the origin episode isn't actually all  that bad as a piece in and of itself  even though it completely wastes Marina  Sirtis  but again I don't even want an origin  for these two and this was absolutely  the wrong place to put it even if it  kind of works in a vacuum  I do want to reiterate that I was  actually pretty pleased with this first  season including the ending but I can't  go into more detail about that without  getting into some very explicit spoilers  so while I've done my best to go spoiler  of light up until now this is your  warning and your cue to bow out if you  don't want the last couple of episodes  of this first season of titans to be  dissected in detail before your eyes I  take you're not clicking off this video  as informed consent here we go the last  two episodes build really well helped  initially by the genuinely creepy vibe  given to the house the characters are in  there's a legitimate haunted house vibe  to the entire proceeding but it's not in  that jump-scare as soon as the jolt ends  the dread fades away kind of thing  no it's slower shots slightly off things  lingering images that disturb the  characters it creeps in without  resorting to trying to startle the  audience for a cheap jump then there's  the turn of Rachel's biological mother  Angela and the appearance of our final  villain I knew pretty much immediately  that the finale was liable to somehow  send her around Rachel's father the  demon Trigon because how else do you  climax a show with Raven moving most of  the plot now I had expected that it  would just end on the reveal of who  Trigon was or maybe the first hint of  his return to Earth be imminent so once  I realized that in fact no we're gonna  get him on screen like right now I  immediately got nervous because up to  this point the show had been extremely  economical with his special effects they  weren't used excessively often and they  you know they got the job done well and  because they were never too ambitious  for the budget but trigon a guy  generally depicted like this I really  dreaded a worst case Scorpion King style  faceplant and the finale is the worst  place of that kind of thing to happen so  I was surprised again when he ended up  being for now at least just a guy I  loved it he actually do a slight  disappointment on that on when it first  happened because despite my fear that it  was going to be a special-effects  faceplant I had kind of hoped to be  surprised by a bonkers visualization and  this made it look like well they're not  even trying  but then he started to speak and I got  on board pretty quickly  the casting works for me because I knew  this actor from the three or four years  that I watched the TV show Castle and on  that he had played a recurring  supporting role as a police detective  the guy's got a very likable presence  which is perfect for what the show is  doing the Trigon here because the show  isn't doing the standard generic final  evil villain spread darkness with his  arrival as some kind of big black  nebulous cloud of evil no it's a  metaphorical darkness  less tangible but no less destructive  Trigon here is a manipulator he's a  Corrupter and having an actor with an  inherent likability factor is perfect  for this this portrayal as well as the  villainous terms of Rachael's mother  also brought back that sinister  domesticity which was lost when the  villains were being led by that tool I  mentioned before now it's not quite on  the level of that assassin family but  Angela doesn't lose her softness when  her evil roots are revealed which is  another wonderful avoidance of a trope  that I loathe the secret villain who  wants their villainy is revealed to the  audience possibly to the heroes not  always behaves completely differently  from how they had up to that point it  happens so much it's  especially in superhero stories and I  get that when they're pretending to be  good that it's all an act but it's  usually such a drastic characterization  shift that the results feel less like a  twist in the same character and more  like the character was flat-out replaced  by somebody else that's not the case  with Rachel's mother she retains that  softness and affection even as she kills  a man he gets ready to help bring the  end of the world and Trigon himself is  not far off from being on the same line  he's playing the good cop he wants to  get you to trust him even as he gets you  to do something completely a guest  against your own best self-interest and  that's what I really love Trigon isn't  infecting or overriding people's free  wills with darkness he's putting them  into circumstances that will cause them  to voluntarily choose that darkness for  themselves it's done slightly heavy  handedly with using gars imminent death  to con Rachel and to bringing Trigon  into the world but after he's here he's  not cackling about being free at last he  embraces Angela and does in fact save  gar then there's what happens to dick so  the last episode also appears at first  to be disconnected from the one  immediately before what  unlike the stupid Hawk and Dove Origin  episode  it's a disconnect that fits because  given that the previous episode ended  would dick basically crossing into the  house where Trigon now has entered onto  our plane of existence having the next  episode opened with him happily married  to dawn aka dove with one child and  another on the way sets off about 50  alarm bells but this is an unreal  artificial and heavily manipulated  situation the show knows better than to  play that as a twist or a surprise it's  pretty obvious from the get-go and that  was very smart  so instead of trying to pull it do a rug  pool with it it used uses the episode to  give us a deep dive into how Trigon  operates how he corrupts and how he gets  people to choose the darkness because  that is technically what dick does he  makes that choice for himself but it's  also very clear that Trigon is  manipulating the situation to push dick  to that point the choice of darkness  versus light is done through the lens of  dick going back to Gotham to confront a  Batman who's completely lost his moral  tether and is on a rampage and every  time it looks like dick is gonna make  the choice that will take him further  away from the darkest possible outcome  the world wobbles a bit and then  something happens that changes the  situation and makes turning away all the  harder I love this for multiple reasons  firstly it's classic lawful evil Trigon  isn't lying when he tells Rachel that  dick chose the darkness but he omits  exactly how much nudging he had to do in  order to get dick to make that choice  and it it was a lot Batman putting  criminals in the hospital doesn't drive  our hero to the edge okay now he's gonna  kill the Joker practically in front of  dick not enough okay  now he's gonna kill every inmate guard  and the warden of Arkham Asylum still  not enough to get dick to go all the way  dark okay now he's gonna murder cops and  then quarry there it is that's enough  that when dick approaches a fallen  Batman reaching out for help he steps on  his neck  and kills him he was pushed enough that  he willingly submits to the darkness  but beyond the insight into trigons  style of manipulation I love how this  speaks to the themes of the difficulty  in  growing up and becoming an adult because  while Rachel Garr and even to a certain  extent Cory are confronting the issues  of teenagers dick is the adult in the  room but that doesn't make his life any  less 